@page "/form-choice"

<CatHead Title="Checks and switches">
    <code>.form-check</code> wraps a checkbox or a radio, <code>.switch</code> is the flip that applies
    at once. All three are a native <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> with a class on the label, so none of
    them needs ARIA.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Controls">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.Checkboxes" Title="Checkboxes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            The whole label toggles the box. The check mark is drawn in CSS, so it picks up the brand
            token.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.HalfTicked" Title="Half ticked" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live.</strong> A &ldquo;select all&rdquo; over a list some of whose rows are ticked is filled
            with a dash. The platform's <code>indeterminate</code> property says so &mdash;
            <code>data-check-all</code> keeps it in step with the rows, see
            <a href="/form-check-list">checkbox groups</a> &mdash; and so does <code>aria-checked="mixed"</code>,
            which an app that renders the state itself writes in the markup. Every checkbox the library draws
            has the state: a table's row selection, a menu's choices, a tree's branches.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.RadioButtons" Title="Radio buttons" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            The same wrapper &mdash; the <code>type</code> decides the shape. A radio is an unfilled circle
            with a brand dot rather than a filled disc, so which one is exclusive reads without colour.
            Give every radio in a set the same <code>name</code> and wrap the set in a
            <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code>, so its legend is announced with each option.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.Switches" Title="Switches" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.switch</code> is for a setting that takes effect the moment it is flipped, so there is
            no <strong>Save</strong> to wait for.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.CheckboxOrSwitch" Title="Checkbox or switch" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>The difference is when it applies, not how it looks.</strong> Pick the wrong one and
            the reader is misled about whether <strong>Save</strong> is still needed &mdash; the same
            setting, drawn both ways.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.ReadOnly" Title="Read-only" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>aria-readonly="true"</code> on a checkbox, a switch, a select, a combo, a segmented
            group, a range or a colour shows its value at full contrast and stops the pointer and the
            keyboard changing it, while it can still be focused and read. A text field takes
            <code>readonly</code> itself. Disabled is different: it says the control does not apply.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Hints and tiles">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.RadioCards" Title="Options with hints" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.choice-text</code> takes the place of the label text and <code>.choice-hint</code> goes
            inside it, so the hint starts where the label does. The same slot works in a
            <code>.form-check</code> and a <code>.switch</code>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.Tiles" Title="Choice tiles" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.choice-tiles</code> is a radio group whose options are pictures — a layout, a plan, a
            theme. Each <code>.choice-tile</code> is a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> around a real radio, so
            the arrows move between options and a form posts the value with no script. The picture in
            <code>.choice-tile-art</code> is yours; <code>.choice-tile-title</code> and
            <code>.choice-tile-hint</code> say what it is. <code>--choice-min</code> is the narrowest a
            tile gets before the row wraps.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Dependent options">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.DependentOptions" Title="Dependent blocks" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Put a <code>.choice-dependent</code> straight after the checkbox, radio or switch it belongs to.
            It indents by that control's inset, <code>--choice-inset-check</code> or
            <code>--choice-inset-switch</code>, so its fields line up with the label, and one inside another
            indents again. A hint between the control and the block breaks the pairing &mdash; put it in
            <code>.choice-hint</code>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormChoice.InactiveOptions" Title="Dimmed or hidden blocks" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Opt in with <code>.choice-dependent--dim</code>, which fades the block and makes it inert, or
            <code>.choice-dependent--hide</code>. Both follow the control's live state, so they need no
            round trip. Without either, the block stays as it is.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
